<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-16"?>
<!--
  SUNI as a Windows scheduled task - the counterpart of deploy/suni.service.

  Import:
    schtasks /Create /TN SUNI /XML deploy\suni-task-windows.xml
  or, in PowerShell:
    Register-ScheduledTask -TaskName SUNI -Xml (Get-Content deploy\suni-task-windows.xml -Raw)

  EDIT FIRST. Every <UserId> and <Author> reads REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-USER, and
  <Command> reads REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-SUNI-PATH. Windows will refuse the
  import until the user is a real account on the machine, and the task will
  do nothing useful until the path points at your clone. Nothing here is
  hardcoded to one install directory, for the same reason start_suni.bat
  resolves its own location: a clone lives wherever its owner put it.

  THE THREE SETTINGS THAT MATTER, and why. All three were wrong on the machine
  this was taken from, and together they produced a THREE-DAY silent outage on
  28 Aug 2026.

  1. A REPEATING TIME TRIGGER, every 10 minutes.
     Restart-on-failure does NOT apply to a task that was TERMINATED rather than
     crashed, and logon/boot triggers never fire again on a machine that simply
     stays up. With only those two triggers nothing was ever going to bring SUNI
     back; it stayed down until a human happened to ask. This trigger is what
     bounds an outage at ten minutes instead of "until somebody notices".

     Firing it while SUNI is already running is safe because start_suni.bat
     declines when the port is already bound. Without that guard a repeating
     trigger would start a second instance over the same memory/ directory -
     two writers on one set of SQLite files, and whichever loses the port bind
     dies noisily while the operator sees a task that "ran fine".

  2. ExecutionTimeLimit = PT0S (no limit).
     The Windows default of PT72H TERMINATES a long-running server every three
     days, forever, on a schedule. It did not cause the outage above - that run
     was 28 hours old - but it guarantees a future one.

  3. StopOnIdleEnd = false.
     Only honoured when RunOnlyIfIdle is true, so it is usually inert. "Usually
     inert" is not a property worth relying on for a setting that stops a server
     when somebody touches the keyboard.

  One more thing, worth doing on the host and NOT expressible in this file:

    wevtutil sl Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational /e:true

  That log is disabled by default. Without it a task termination cannot be
  attributed to anything afterwards - which is precisely the wall the 28 Aug
  investigation hit: the task's LastTaskResult proved it had been terminated,
  and nothing anywhere could say by what.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Date>2026-09-01T00:00:00</Date>
    <Author>REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-USER</Author>
    <URI>\SUNI</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-USER</UserId>
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>HighestAvailable</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Hidden>true</Hidden>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Count>5</Count>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
    </RestartOnFailure>
    <IdleSettings>
      <Duration>PT10M</Duration>
      <WaitTimeout>PT1H</WaitTimeout>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
  </Settings>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2026-04-20T16:48:00</StartBoundary>
    </LogonTrigger>
    <BootTrigger>
      <Delay>PT2M</Delay>
    </BootTrigger>
    <TimeTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2026-09-01T00:01:00+01:00</StartBoundary>
      <Repetition>
        <Interval>PT10M</Interval>
        <StopAtDurationEnd>true</StopAtDurationEnd>
      </Repetition>
    </TimeTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-SUNI-PATH\start_suni.bat</Command>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
